1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:05,640
A robot built to last ninety days on Mars
kept going for more than fourteen years.

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:09,560
Why did it survive so long,
and what finally stopped it?

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,700
Mars does not crush machines.

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:14,200
It does not melt them.

5
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:16,619
It wears them down, slowly,

6
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:21,320
in ways you already know from home:
the cold that kills a phone battery,

7
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,720
the dust on a windscreen,
a tyre that finally splits.

8
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:28,460
So could a robot survive on Mars?

9
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,040
Could Orbit?

10
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:34,420
In this film I will show you what
Mars does to the machines we send,

11
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:38,020
how some of them lasted far
longer than anyone planned,

12
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:40,820
and what it really takes
to stay alive there.

13
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:45,540
At first glance, Mars looks like
a desert you could drive across.

14
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:48,960
Red rock, rolling dunes, a hazy sky.

15
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:50,920
It is a trick of the eye.

16
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,740
The air is so thin that
the pressure at the

17
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:56,700
ground is less than
one percent of Earth's.

18
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:00,840
The average temperature is
about minus sixty degrees.

19
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:06,400
At the first Viking landing site,
nights fell to nearly minus ninety.

20
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:11,500
And because the air is thin and
Mars has no global magnetic shield,

21
00:01:11,540 --> 00:01:13,740
radiation reaches the ground.

22
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:15,900
Curiosity measured it.

23
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:22,280
In one day on Mars, the dose is roughly what
you would get in three months on Earth.

24
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,120
A person would need a pressure
suit just to stand there.

25
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:30,980
But a robot does not breathe,
and it does not get sick.

26
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:37,540
So the thin air and the radiation are not
what usually ends a robot's life on Mars.

27
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:39,960
The killers are quieter.

28
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:41,860
Cold.

29
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:43,340
Dust.

30
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:44,940
Wear.

31
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:48,460
Of the robots that landed
safely and went to work,

32
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:51,080
most were stopped by one of those three.

33
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:54,860
And whatever goes wrong,
nobody can rush to help.

34
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:58,880
A radio signal takes from
about three minutes to more

35
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:03,060
than twenty to cross between
the planets, each way.

36
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:07,540
Nobody can steer a rover
live, like a toy car.

37
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:13,300
Every robot on Mars has to look after
itself between messages from home.

38
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:17,080
Start with the cold, because
you have felt what it does.

39
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:21,280
Take your phone out on a freezing
day and watch the battery drop.

40
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:24,380
Electronics and batteries hate deep cold.

41
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:29,880
On Mars, every night is colder than
any winter night in a British garden.

42
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:34,480
So a rover spends part of its power
just keeping its own insides warm,

43
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:37,240
like a person burning energy to shiver.

44
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:40,720
That only works while
power keeps coming in.

45
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:45,880
In 2009 the rover Spirit
drove into soft sand at

46
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:49,560
a place the team called
Troy, and its wheels sank.

47
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:51,440
It could not climb out.

48
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:55,880
It could not tilt its solar panels
towards the low winter Sun.

49
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:59,380
As the days shortened, its power fell.

50
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:05,600
On the twenty-second of March
2010, Spirit sent its last message.

51
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:08,400
Spirit did not break apart.

52
00:03:08,620 --> 00:03:12,000
It got stuck, and winter did the rest.

53
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:15,600
Its mission had been
planned for ninety days.

54
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:18,280
It worked for more than six years.

55
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:21,860
The cold can be even more direct.

56
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:28,320
In 2008 the Phoenix lander touched down
near the north pole of Mars to dig for ice.

57
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:31,460
When winter came, it went silent.

58
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:36,200
Two years later, a spacecraft
in orbit photographed it again.

59
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:38,620
Its solar panels looked broken,

60
00:03:38,660 --> 00:03:44,040
most likely bent by the weight of frost
and ice that had built up over the winter.

61
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:47,260
We make one of these every week.

62
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:50,620
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

63
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:54,520
Spirit had a twin on the far side of Mars.

64
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:58,580
It lasted much longer, and
the reason was the wind.

65
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:01,080
Mars is a dusty world.

66
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:05,260
Fine red dust settles on
everything, including solar panels.

67
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:07,500
A panel under dust is like a windscreen

68
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:10,880
nobody wipes: less and
less light gets through.

69
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:14,540
A rover that runs on
sunlight slowly starves.

70
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:17,299
Opportunity should have
starved within months.

71
00:04:17,339 --> 00:04:18,940
Instead, again and again,

72
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:23,380
Martian wind and passing dust
devils swept its panels clean.

73
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:26,020
The team called them cleaning events.

74
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:29,740
Each one gave the rover back
its power, and it kept driving.

75
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,100
Ninety days became years.

76
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:34,480
Years became more than fourteen.

77
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:39,420
Then, in 2018, a storm grew until
it wrapped the whole planet.

78
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:45,260
The sky over Opportunity grew so dark that
almost no sunlight reached its panels.

79
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:50,500
On the tenth of June 2018,
Opportunity sent its last signal.

80
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:53,720
Engineers on Earth sent more
than a thousand commands

81
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:56,840
over the next eight months,
asking it to wake up.

82
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:58,540
It never answered.

83
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:04,720
What finally stopped the most successful
rover of its time was not cold or rock.

84
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:07,220
It was the sky turning off.

85
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:10,520
Why does dust matter so much?

86
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:14,400
Because on Mars there is no
rain to wash anything clean.

87
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:18,100
The lander InSight spent four
years listening for marsquakes,

88
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:21,080
and felt more than a thousand of them.

89
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:23,760
But dust kept building on its panels.

90
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:30,900
In December 2022, it went quiet the same
way: not broken, just out of light.

91
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:34,440
Even a robot that never runs
out of power wears out.

92
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:37,960
Curiosity runs on a nuclear power source,

93
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:43,720
a small supply of plutonium that gives
off heat day and night, dust or no dust.

94
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:47,820
When the 2018 storm
darkened Opportunity's sky,

95
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:51,060
Curiosity kept working in
another part of the planet.

96
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:53,240
But its wheels had a different problem.

97
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:57,800
They are thin aluminium, made
light enough to fly to Mars.

98
00:05:57,980 --> 00:06:00,940
In 2013, the team noticed holes.

99
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:05,320
Sharp rocks were punching through,
like a tyre on broken glass.

100
00:06:05,744 --> 00:06:09,984
Since then the drivers have planned
routes around the sharpest ground,

101
00:06:10,044 --> 00:06:13,184
and they photograph the wheels
to watch the damage grow.

102
00:06:13,784 --> 00:06:15,804
Why not just build the wheels tougher?

103
00:06:16,164 --> 00:06:20,664
Because every kilogram has to be
launched off Earth, carried for months,

104
00:06:20,684 --> 00:06:25,804
slowed from thousands of kilometres an
hour and set down gently on another world.

105
00:06:25,964 --> 00:06:30,624
On Mars, strength always has to be
weighed against getting there at all.

106
00:06:31,264 --> 00:06:33,464
And then there is the little helicopter.

107
00:06:33,924 --> 00:06:38,304
Ingenuity was built for up to five
test flights over thirty days,

108
00:06:38,304 --> 00:06:41,104
in air one percent as thick as ours.

109
00:06:41,444 --> 00:06:45,244
It flew seventy-two times
over almost three years.

110
00:06:45,624 --> 00:06:52,364
In January 2024, after its last landing,
images showed damage to its rotor blades.

111
00:06:52,504 --> 00:06:54,164
It could not fly again.

112
00:06:54,624 --> 00:06:56,644
It is still standing on Mars.

113
00:06:57,124 --> 00:07:00,004
So, could a robot survive on Mars?

114
00:07:00,264 --> 00:07:01,264
Yes.

115
00:07:01,264 --> 00:07:03,304
Not forever, and not alone.

116
00:07:03,624 --> 00:07:07,584
The robots that last are the ones
with a steady supply of power,

117
00:07:07,644 --> 00:07:09,424
heaters to fight the night,

118
00:07:09,424 --> 00:07:13,704
and a team on Earth who drive
carefully around every sharp rock.

119
00:07:14,144 --> 00:07:19,284
Sometimes they also need luck, in the shape
of a gust of wind at the right moment.

120
00:07:19,564 --> 00:07:22,004
That is what Mars teaches.

121
00:07:22,124 --> 00:07:25,264
Survival there is not one heroic moment.

122
00:07:25,324 --> 00:07:31,324
It is a thousand small decisions, made
from another planet, every single day.

123
00:07:31,884 --> 00:07:33,344
What would Orbit need?

124
00:07:33,544 --> 00:07:36,504
Power that does not depend on a clear sky.

125
00:07:36,624 --> 00:07:39,164
A way to stay warm through the night.

126
00:07:39,284 --> 00:07:42,124
A shell tough enough for sharp ground.

127
00:07:42,244 --> 00:07:44,664
And someone at home, watching.

128
00:07:45,024 --> 00:07:49,864
What if the real story of every
Mars robot is not how it died,

129
00:07:49,904 --> 00:07:52,464
but how long people kept it going?

130
00:07:52,664 --> 00:07:55,744
The bigger question sits
in the tracks they leave.

131
00:07:55,964 --> 00:08:00,704
Every robot that stops on Mars
stays exactly where it stopped,

132
00:08:00,704 --> 00:08:03,264
and the dust slowly covers it.

133
00:08:03,804 --> 00:08:06,704
Next week: how far is the nearest star?
